  "content": {
    "id": "p3-q11",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "How many ways can the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 be arranged in a row so that",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p3-q11a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "the product of any two adjacent numbers is even",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p3-q11a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "144",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p3-q11a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Separate the three odd numbers using three of the four gaps around the even numbers.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "A product is odd only when both adjacent numbers are odd, so no two of 1, 3 and 5 may be adjacent.\nArrange 2, 4 and 6 in $3\\times2\\times1=6$ ways. There are four gaps: before, between and after these even numbers. Leave one gap empty in 4 ways, then arrange the three odd numbers in the other gaps in $3\\times2\\times1=6$ ways.\n$$6\\times4\\times6=144$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p3-q11b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "the sum of any two adjacent numbers is greater than 6?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p3-q11b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "8",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p3-q11b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "List four valid arrangements starting with 1, then include their reversals.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The only possible neighbour of 1 is 6, so 1 must be at an end. First place 1 at the left end, followed by 6.\nThe number 2 can neighbour only 5 or 6. If 2 is next after 6, it must be followed by 5, leaving 3 and 4 in either order:\n$$1,6,2,5,3,4\\qquad 1,6,2,5,4,3.$$\nOtherwise 2 must be the right endpoint and its neighbour must be 5. The remaining 3 and 4 can occur in either order:\n$$1,6,3,4,5,2\\qquad 1,6,4,3,5,2.$$\nAll four satisfy the condition. Reversing them gives four more arrangements with 1 at the right end.\n$$4+4=8$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      }
    ]
  },
